body:
@Splizard I have no opinions on merits of `bswap`. I can see how it can be useful in general, but it's not useful in my case and for the examples that I have brought to the original proposal. I just feel like merits of `bswap` haven't been sufficiently deliberated/rationalized prior to its addition, though they probably exist and it's a reasonable method to have. But I think that its existence is largely unrelated to the proposal and mere existence of the proposal shouldn't have been grounds by itself to add the method. Again, as far as I am involved in the proposal at least.

Adding 18, or however much, methods is only a necessity to avoid breaking compatibility by introducing an argument to existing methods. That just seems like the least invasive approach. For my purpose, it could've been solved by adding an instance member to mark the entire array as BE, and adjust decoding and encoding internally accordingly. Like the file API does it. But that's not possible because packed arrays are just typedefs for `Vector<T>`. And it seems some data formats can embed BE data in LE document, where this won't work at all.

If the issue moves nowhere, it's ultimately not a big deal. Even if performance of the reverse method is crap, one can probably make a GDExtension to build the array more efficiently. It is pretty disappointing though to not be able to work with BE byte arrays when you can work with BE files.
